      "doc_id": "nwss_wval",
      "heading": "Using the Wastewater Viral Activity Level > Understanding the WVAL",
      "text": "CDC developed the wastewater viral activity level (WVAL) metric to create a standardized method for comparing wastewater data within and across sites. This metric compares current levels to low levels of a virus at a site over the last 24 months.\n\nCDC has validated the WVAL methodology for the following common respiratory illnesses: Influenza A, COVID-19, and RSV."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_wval#0",
      "doc_id": "nwss_wval",
      "heading": "Using the Wastewater Viral Activity Level > What to know",
      "text": "- CDC developed the wastewater viral activity level (WVAL) to compare the current levels of common respiratory viruses to low viral levels at that location over the last 24 months.\n- WVALs allow data to be compared between sites, states/territories, regions, and nationally."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_readme#16",
      "doc_id": "oad_readme",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData README > Important caveats",
      "text": "- **Wide tables have no revision history.** They hold only the latest value for each week.\n- **ED \"regions\" are CDC health service areas (HSAs).** These are multi-county units: every county in an HSA shares one reported number.\n- **Sites are the only valid geography for wastewater.** A wastewater site can serve several counties, and some serve counties in more than one state.\n- **Wastewater activity levels can't be compared across pathogens.** Use `wastewater_activity_category` to compare.\n- **The flag thresholds are data-derived, not clinically validated.** See [METHODS.md](METHODS.md) for how they were set, and for the known limitations."
    }
  ]
