    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#11",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "- Using X = percent of ED visits in a week for each pathogen and ARI overall, we classified activity levels as follows:\n  - Very Low: X \\<= M, activity at or below baseline\n  - Low: M \\< X \\<= M + D\n  - Moderate: M + D \\< X \\<= M + 2D\n  - High: M + 2D \\< X \\<= + 3D\n  - Very High: M + 3D \\< X\n- **Missouri:** Due to data quality issues, Missouri's historical data before MMWR Week 10 of 2025 (March 2-8, 2025) could not be used for computing levels, thus leaving insufficient data. To address this, data from the rest of HHS Region 7 (Iowa, Kansas, and Nebraska) were used in place of Missouri's own before Week 10 of 2025 to compute the 2026-2027 levels for Missouri; for Week 10 of 2025 and later in the historical baseline years, Missouri's data was used."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#63",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "- What: We utilize the ARI metric to categorize the level of respiratory illnesses as Minimal, Low, Moderate, High, or Very High in two places:\n  - 'Your community snapshot' on the main page of the Respiratory Illness Data Channel alongside other state and community data.\n  - Respiratory Virus Activity Levels, where we replaced the map based on influenza-like illness (ILI) with a map using the new ARI metric to capture the level of respiratory illness activity across the US. ILI data continues to be available on the ILINet State Activity Indicator Map.\n- Why:\n  - The ARI metric provides a broader picture than ILI for evaluating overall respiratory illness trends. Some respiratory illnesses do not have fever as a main symptom and are not captured consistently in measures using ILI, which uses fever as a criterion.\n  - The new summary metric characterizes overall ARI activity to monitor the combined effects of multiple respiratory illnesses and when used in combination with laboratory confirmed pathogen specific surveillance can help monitor trends in illness associated with respiratory pathogens."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#2",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Overall respiratory illness activity",
      "text": "Explanation:\n\n- Respiratory illness activity is monitored using the acute respiratory illness (ARI) definition. ARI captures a broad range of emergency department visits for respiratory illnesses, ranging from the common cold to potentially severe illnesses like flu (influenza), RSV and COVID-19. It captures a broader range of respiratory illnesses, including those that do not present with fever, compared to the previously shown influenza-like illness (ILI) metric (Note: the ILI metric is still being used for flu monitoring).\n- Respiratory illness activity levels are based on comparing the percent of emergency department visits for ARI during the current week to the average percent of visits during times when COVID-19, flu and RSV visits were low (Note: other respiratory illness-related visits may still be occurring). These low points are considered \"baseline\" levels of respiratory illness activity The activity level is based on how elevated the current week is from the \"baseline\" period. See Activity Levels data notes for the detailed methodology.\n- Emergency department data are collected through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program."
    }
  ]
}

