    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_about_data#5",
      "doc_id": "nwss_about_data",
      "heading": "About Wastewater Data > Limitations",
      "text": "Most jurisdictions report data from municipal systems in urban and suburban areas. Communities that rely more on non-sewered sanitation systems (such as septic tanks), or that are in rural areas, might not participate in wastewater monitoring. As a result, national, regional, or state data may not represent the full picture of viral activity for the region.\n\nIt is challenging to use wastewater data to estimate the number of people in a community who are sick. To accurately calculate these estimates, data are needed to understand how much virus is shed from the body over the course of infection, and how that is impacted by disease severity, different strains of a virus, and other factors.\n\nSpecific wastewater data limitations for each pathogen are available in the data footnotes or About the Data sections:\n\nThis page was last updated on this date. Updates may include minor edits, image changes, or other modifications to page content.\n\nThe information on this page was last reviewed by subject matter experts to ensure accuracy."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#16",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData methods > 5. Known limitations",
      "text": "1. **The flag thresholds are provisional.** They are data-derived and have not been externally or clinically validated. Treat the flags as screening aids, not ground truth.\n2. **Coverage flags are static for ED and wastewater.** The NSSP participation status reflects a single August 2026 facility map. The NWSS participation status is one summary per site and pathogen in each source release. Neither describes how coverage changed over time.\n3. **Some region IDs repeat across region types.** `analysis_region_id` is unique only together with `analysis_region_type`. In Pennsylvania, the HSA with code `42` and the state FIPS code `42` coexist.\n4. **Source coverage varies by state and period.**\n   - NSSP data begin in October 2022.\n   - The NSSP `combined` series ends in September 2024 in most states.\n   - NHSN influenza reporting in most states begins in October 2020.\n   - North Dakota has no influenza A or RSV wastewater data. Its COVID-19 wastewater data (2 sites) end on 2023-09-16."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_national#5",
      "doc_id": "nwss_national",
      "heading": "National Wastewater Data for Respiratory Viruses > About the data > Limited/No data",
      "text": "States or territories without sufficient data to estimate the wastewater viral activity level for the previous week are indicated as \"Limited/No Data.\" A label of \"Limited/No Data\" means that either:\n\n- No data were reported this week from sites within the state or territory\n- Data were reported this week, but sites within the state or territory do not have enough data to estimate the wastewater viral activity level in the last week."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#4",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Wastewater viral activity level",
      "text": "Explanation:\n\n- Wastewater data are collected through the National Wastewater Surveillance System (NWSS). Wastewater monitoring can detect viruses spreading in a community whether or not people have symptoms. It does not depend on people having access to healthcare or visiting a doctor for testing. Increased wastewater viral activity levels may indicate increased risk of infection in an area.\n- Wastewater data may show changes in disease trends earlier than data from clinical cases. For example, wastewater data can be used as an early indicator for monitoring the spread of infection before people have symptoms or require medical care. Therefore, wastewater levels may go up (or down) before we see changes in metrics like emergency department use. Wastewater surveillance can complement other existing surveillance systems to monitor respiratory viruses.\n- Wastewater testing for influenza cannot determine the source of the influenza viruses detected. The viruses could come from a human or from an animal (like a bird) or an animal product (like milk from an infected cow); therefore, these detections do not necessarily indicate human cases."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#5",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Wastewater viral activity level",
      "text": "- Wastewater testing for influenza displayed in these visuals only detects influenza A viruses and does not distinguish between influenza A subtypes e.g. seasonal influenza A(H1N1), seasonal influenza A(H3N2) and avian influenza A(H5).\n- Wastewater data will not include waste that may be disposed of outside the wastewater system, such as from people wearing diapers. This likely explains why trends in wastewater data for RSV do not always align with emergency department visits for RSV, which causes severe disease primarily in infants under 6 months and older adults.\n- States or territories with limited coverage are those where wastewater monitoring covers a small segment (less than 5%) of the state or territory population. Because data from limited coverage areas may not fully reflect infections for the larger population, wastewater viral activity levels are not shown on the 'Your community snapshot'. Levels are shown in limited coverage areas with hatch marks on the activity level page. These data are also made available on the National Wastewater Surveillance System homepage."
